<%^hasBackend%>
## 1. Objective
Prove the whole pipeline end to end (code, tests, running system, deploy) with the smallest possible feature: a web page that greets the visitor, by name when the address gives one.

## 2. User stories & acceptance criteria
- **AC-001** Given the web app is running, when a user opens the web root `/`, then the page shows the greeting `Hello, World!`.
- **AC-002** Given the web app is running, when a user opens `/?name=Ada`, then the page shows the greeting `Hello, Ada!`.
- **AC-003** Given the web app is running, when a user opens `/` with a `name` longer than 50 characters, then the name is ignored and the page shows `Hello, World!`.

## 3. Permissions & roles
None: the page is public and read-only.

## 4. Data & validation
- `name`: optional query parameter. Surrounding spaces are removed; it is used only when it is then 1 to 50 characters long. Anything else (missing, blank, longer than 50) is ignored and the greeting is for `World`.
- The greeting is always `Hello, <name>!`.

## 5. Contract impact
None. This project has no separate API: the Next.js app renders the page itself.

## 6. Out of scope
Authentication, persistence, localization, and any API or database.

## 7. Verification map
See `acceptance.json`. All three criteria are proven by HTTP checks against the running web app.
